<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhodesian Bush War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1964">1964</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Second Chimurenga, Zimbabwe War of Liberation · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhodesian Bush War was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war fought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> nationalist movements against the white minority government of Rhodesia. It intensified after 1972, with guerrillas operating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>; tens of thousands died. It ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> Agreement of 1979, which led to majority rule and the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhodesian-bush-war.jpg" alt="Rhodesian Bush War" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kelly The Angel · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RhodesiaAllies1975-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war's origins lie in the conquest of the region by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> in the late 19th century and the dissent of African leaders who opposed foreign rule. Britons began settling in Southern Rhodesia in the 1890s, and from 1923 the settlers effectively governed it. Property requirements kept many Black Africans from voting. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a>'s Wind of Change speech <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> would grant independence to its African colonies only under majority rule, and many white Rhodesians feared chaos, as in the former Belgian Congo. Rhodesia's unilateral declaration of independence on 11 November 1965 won no diplomatic recognition, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and Portugal gave private support.</p><p>Nationalists and the British government of the time saw a war of national and racial liberation, and considered the country occupied and dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> since 1890. The Rhodesian government saw a fight by one part of the population on behalf of the whole against externally financed radicals and communists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 4 July 1964 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a> insurgents murdered a white foreman, Pieter Oberholzer, and the Smith government detained ZANU and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> leaders in August. Operating from bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> began attacks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> supported ZIPRA, the armed wing of ZAPU, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> supported ZANLA, the armed wing of ZANU. The two sometimes fought each other. Rhodesia met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> by obtaining oil and arms through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and Portugal.</p><p>After 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> activity grew, especially in the northeast. According to Rhodesian figures, from December 1972 to December 1979, 1,120 members of the security forces, 10,050 guerrillas killed in Rhodesia, 7,790 black civilians and 468 white civilians died. An internal settlement led to the election of 24 April 1979, in which Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-muzorewa">Abel Muzorewa</a> became the first black prime minister of Zimbabwe-Rhodesia. Nkomo's and Mugabe's forces rejected it as a puppet government, and it won no international recognition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in December 1979 between the Zimbabwe-Rhodesian government, the United Kingdom and Mugabe and Nkomo's Patriotic Front produced the Lancaster House Agreement. The country returned temporarily to British control, and elections in March 1980 under British and Commonwealth supervision were won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a>. Robert Mugabe became the first Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> on 18 April 1980. The Rhodesian army was merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> forces. Norma Kriger has questioned the nationalist account of peasant support, and Luise White the claim that the Rhodesian army won every battle but lost at Lancaster House.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhodesian Bush War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
